List Implementations:

// List interface
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

// ArrayList
ArrayList<String> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();

// LinkedList
LinkedList<String> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();

// Vector (legacy, synchronized)
Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();

// Stack (legacy, extends Vector)
Stack<String> stack = new Stack<>();

Set Implementations:

// Set interface
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

// HashSet
HashSet<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>();

// LinkedHashSet (insertion order maintained)
LinkedHashSet<String> linkedHashSet = new LinkedHashSet<>();

// TreeSet (sorted order)
TreeSet<String> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();


Queue Iplementations:

// Queue interface
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();

// LinkedList as Queue
LinkedList<String> queueLinkedList = new LinkedList<>();

// PriorityQueue (min-heap by default)
PriorityQueue<String> priorityQueue = new PriorityQueue<>();


Deque Implementations:

// Deque interface
Deque<String> deque = new ArrayDeque<>();

// ArrayDeque
ArrayDeque<String> arrayDeque = new ArrayDeque<>();

// LinkedList as Deque
LinkedList<String> dequeLinkedList = new LinkedList<>();


Map Implementations:

// Map interface
Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

// HashMap
HashMap<Integer, String> hashMap = new HashMap<>();

// LinkedHashMap (insertion order maintained)
LinkedHashMap<Integer, String> linkedHashMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();

// TreeMap (sorted order of keys)
TreeMap<Integer, String> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();

// Hashtable (legacy, synchronized)
Hashtable<Integer, String> hashtable = new Hashtable<>();


Concurrent Collections:

// CopyOnWriteArrayList (read-heavy, write-light)
CopyOnWriteArrayList<String> cowList = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

// ConcurrentHashMap
ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, String> concurrentHashMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

Summary / Quick Usage:
Use ArrayList for fast random access.
Use LinkedList for frequent insert/remove.
Use HashSet for unique, unordered elements.
Use LinkedHashSet for insertion order.
Use TreeSet for sorted elements.
Use PriorityQueue for priority-based retrieval.
Use ArrayDeque for stack/queue operations.
Use HashMap for fast key-value pairs.
Use LinkedHashMap for predictable iteration.
Use TreeMap for sorted keys.
Use ConcurrentHashMap or CopyOnWriteArrayList for thread safety.




1. Collection Hierarchy

Collection:
    List
        ArrayList
        LinkedList (also implements Deque, Queue)
        Vector (legacy, synchronized)
                Stack (extends Vector, LIFO)
        CopyOnWriteArrayList (thread-safe, concurrent)
    Set
        HashSet
                LinkedHashSet (insertion order)
        TreeSet (sorted set, uses NavigableSet)
    Queue
        LinkedList (FIFO queue, also Deque)
        PriorityQueue (priority-based ordering)
    Deque
        ArrayDeque (double-ended queue)
        LinkedList (also works as deque)

2. Map Hierarchy

Map
  HashMap
    LinkedHashMap (insertion order)
    ConcurrentHashMap (thread-safe, concurrent)
  TreeMap (sorted order of keys, uses NavigableMap)
  Hashtable (legacy, synchronized)

🔹 Legacy vs Modern
Legacy: Vector, Stack, Hashtable.
Modern: ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, HashMap, TreeSet, ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList.
